C cyc_na
"Out-of-thin-air load buffering over the WEAKEST mode (LB+datas). P0 reads x
 into r1 and stores r1 to y; P1 reads y into r2 and stores r2 to x. The outcome
 r1=r2=42 invents 42 from a cyclic justification in po|rf -- no execution ever
 writes 42 except by reading it back round the cycle.

 This is the JAM-allows / JMM-forbids direction (here the relaxed atomics stand
 in for JAM's PLAIN access mode; this is JAM's own cyc_na program).

 JAM (Java Access Modes) ALLOWS it. JAM is modelled on RC11 and adopts RC11's
 no-thin-air fix -- acyclic(po|rf) -- but ONLY for opaque-or-stronger accesses;
 for plain (non-atomic) accesses it deliberately leaves the cycle unconstrained,
 so the plain-mode program keeps the po|rf cycle and the thin-air outcome is
 permitted.

 The original JMM FORBIDS it. The canonical OOTA r=r=42 is the motivating target
 of the JMM's commitment semantics: a value may only be committed if some
 well-behaved execution produces it, and 42 has no such justification, so the
 causality rules reject it. (The JMM's causality machinery is famously flawed in
 OTHER cases -- see LB+opaque.litmus for the reverse direction -- but it does
 forbid this textbook thin-air load-buffering.)

 Verdicts are CITED, not machine-run: there is no shared checker -- the JMM is
 not a herd7 model and JAM's mechanisation is the Coq development accompanying
 the paper. herd7 cannot construct an OOTA execution in any case (it ties reads
 to written values). See README."
{ int x=0; int y=0; }
P0 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {                  /* JAM plain mode */
  int r1 = atomic_load_explicit(x,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(y,r1,memory_order_relaxed);
}
P1 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {                  /* JAM plain mode */
  int r2 = atomic_load_explicit(y,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(x,r2,memory_order_relaxed);
}
exists (0:r1=42 /\ 1:r2=42)
